 "verdict": "physical discovery footprint does not reproduce the arrival dipole direction; the axis-aligned component survives footprint subtraction",
 "caveats": [
  "forward model is first-principles: circular Earth orbit, Kepler conic time law, sharp observability cuts and a 1/(r^2 D^2) brightness proxy; it is not a survey-realistic pipeline",
  "the footprint dipole direction is stable across the scenario grid for the class-1 cohort but its amplitude is weighting-scheme dependent",
  "the resampled null holds each comet's real perihelion epoch fixed; epoch marginals are near-uniform (Ls dipole R~0.06)",
  "The full-cohort keys are retained for compatibility, but their n is the visibility-model-eligible subset; conclusions do not extend to excluded objects",
  "Orientation weights are normalized per object; the null holds the observed q/e/epoch cohort fixed, rather than reweighting its brightness distribution a second time",
  "Robustness scenarios use only objects eligible under each scenario; the object count can change",
  "The matched-q subset coincides with the reference eligible class-1 subset and is not an independent replication",
  "Finite orientation quadrature is approximate; null resampling error does not include quadrature or survey-model uncertainty",
  "results are for the dipole (l=1) mode; higher multipoles are not tested here"
 ]
}